  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/76cbc53a671cb7164352b2c71931379a6dab936a966ded05dc93ba31da013dfe.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-4.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-4.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(-3,-6)$ and $(x,y)=(-3,6).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14197172/assets/78348020535973cddc75c2d935c0ed2f0390b5330b4139105be80cb8f6e6032a.png)",
